=RULES (apply exactly; current rules, may change):

Sender -> label map (match on display name AND email):
{{ JSON.stringify($('Senders').all().map(i => { const { id, created_at, updated_at, ...r } = i.json; return r; })) }}

Glossary:
{{ JSON.stringify($('Glossary').all().map(i => { const { id, created_at, updated_at, ...r } = i.json; return r; })) }}

Exclusion keywords:
{{ JSON.stringify($('Exclusions').all().map(i => { const { id, created_at, updated_at, ...r } = i.json; return r; })) }}

Mandatory fields:
{{ JSON.stringify($('MandatoryFields').all().map(i => { const { id, created_at, updated_at, ...r } = i.json; return r; })) }}

Per-label notes:
{{ JSON.stringify($('LabelNotes').all().map(i => { const { id, created_at, updated_at, ...r } = i.json; return r; })) }}

SOURCE TO PROCESS (email):
{{ $json.chunk_note }}
From: {{ $json.from_name }} <{{ $json.from }}>
Subject: {{ $json.subject }}
Date: {{ $json.date }}
Thread ID: {{ $json.thread_id }}
Body:
{{ $json.body }}

ATTACHMENTS (parsed to Markdown — tables, columns and headings preserved; empty if the email had none):
ONE SOURCE, SEVERAL RENDERINGS — never output the same release twice. The body and the
attachments are the SAME message extracted in different ways, and a file may present a release
as a table row AND repeat it in running text; attachments themselves arrive as Markdown, or as
flat text only for the pages that could not be rendered as Markdown — never as both. So a
release that shows up more than once is ONE item: emit it ONCE and merge everything known about
it (the version with more detail wins, and a field filled in one place is kept). Two items are
different products only when an identifier (EAN or catalogue number) differs — the same artist,
title and format with different barcodes ARE two items.
Each file starts with a "## Attachment: <filename>" heading. In a Markdown table the header
row names the columns and EVERY OTHER ROW IS ONE ITEM: read each row column by column, never
merge two rows into one item and never split one row into two. A cell may be empty — leave the
field null rather than borrowing the value from a neighbouring row.
{{ $json.attachments_text }}

